  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 211 PM MST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nSatellite imagery shows an elongated trough axis working across\nIdaho today, streaming predominantly mid- and high-level cloud \ncover over eastern Idaho. Deterministic models continue to \nresolve very isolated showers over portions of the central \nmountains and the Montana Divide. In addition, recent high-res \nCAM runs (RRFS/HRRR/REFS) have initiated a few light showers \nacross the southeast corner late this afternoon into early this \nevening. Expected QPF remains negligible to trace amounts, so \ndespite snow levels hovering around 7,000\u20138,000 feet, impacts \nwill be minimal to non-existent. Behind the departing trough \naxis, an upper-level ridge builds back across the region for the\nweekend. This will establish dry, pleasant fall conditions with\nmostly sunny skies, generally light winds, and a steady warming\ntrend, pushing daytime highs back above normal once again.\n\nA weak dry shortwave passes to the north on Sunday, momentarily\nflattening the ridge. The only sensible impact expected from \nthis feature is a minor bump in afternoon wind speeds across \nexposed terrain and the Snake River Plain. High temperatures \nwill continue to run roughly 5 to 10 degrees above seasonal \nnorms into early next week, reaching the 50s and lower 60s \nacross much of Southeast Idaho.\n\nA similar weak wave approaches on Tuesday, potentially\nsupporting a low-end shower chance across far northern zones.\nConfidence remains very low and dry conditions will largely\nprevail for most of the region. Looking further out, ensemble\nmeans favor the upper ridge breaking down during the latter half\nof the week into the weekend, introducing an increasing chance\nof showers across the region by Thursday. Ensemble clusters are\nsplit between maintaining an amplified ridge over the\nIntermountain West and pushing the trough inland. There is a lot\nof room for fluctuation in either direction by next weekend.",
